\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15.1}{\ignorespaces Why residual graphs have backward edges. All capacities are 1. The first augmenting path uses $a \to b$ and blocks both remaining paths; in the residual graph, the backward edge $b \to a$ (dashed) lets the second path $s, b, a, t$ reroute the first unit, giving the maximum flow 2.}}{194}{figure.caption.250}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:residual}{{15.1}{194}{Why residual graphs have backward edges. All capacities are 1. The first augmenting path uses $a \to b$ and blocks both remaining paths; in the residual graph, the backward edge $b \to a$ (dashed) lets the second path $s, b, a, t$ reroute the first unit, giving the maximum flow 2}{figure.caption.250}{}}
